Short:        Converts OFS/FFS partitions to PFS3
Author:       terriblefire
Uploader:     stephen@terriblefire.com (terriblefire)
Type:         disk/misc
Version:      0.3
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 2.0.4
Requires:     a hard disk with an RDB

ffs2pfs
=======

ffs2pfs converts an OFS or FFS hard disk partition to PFS3 in place,
keeping every file. It is built to survive a power cut at any moment:
after one, it either finishes the conversion or puts the partition back
to the FFS volume it was.

  - Files, directories, hard and soft links, comments, protection bits
    and dates are kept.
  - The partition's RDB entry gets pfs3aio (the PFS3 handler, built into
    ffs2pfs) and the DosType PFS\3, so the disk mounts and boots as PFS3
    with no other disk needed.
  - Every step is journalled on the disk itself. A conversion that was
    cut off is found again from the disk alone and carries on from where
    it stopped.
  - If anything fails, the volume is rolled back to FFS, exactly as it
    was.
  - A window (Workbench) or the Shell, on any 68000 or better with
    Kickstart 2.04 or later.


Back up first
-------------

ffs2pfs rewrites the partition on the disk itself. It is built to keep
every file and to undo its work when something fails, but a disk fault,
a bug, or another program writing to the disk can still lose data. Back
up the partition before you convert it: once a conversion has finished,
the only way back to FFS is your backup.

Afterwards the partition needs PFS3. Amigas mount it with the pfs3aio in
its RDB; other systems may not read it.


Files
-----

  ffs2pfs/ffs2pfs            the program (window and Shell)
  ffs2pfs/pfs3aio.LICENSE    the licence of pfs3aio, which is built in
  ffs2pfs.readme             this readme

Copy ffs2pfs to C: (or anywhere). To start it from Workbench, give it a
tool icon, or use Show > All Files in its drawer.


The window
----------

Started from Workbench, or as "ffs2pfs" with no arguments in a Shell,
ffs2pfs opens a window. It lists the partitions of every disk with an
RDB, and where each one stands:

  can be converted      OFS or FFS, not the boot volume
  boot volume           OFS or FFS, the boot volume
  stopped at step n/6   a conversion was cut off (power cut, Stop)
  rollback cut off      a rollback was cut off
  last write left       past the point of no return: Convert finishes it
  PFS3                  PFS3 already: nothing to do
  not OFS/FFS           another file system: nothing to do

Pick a partition, then:

  Analyse      checks the volume and the free space it needs, writes
               nothing
  Convert      converts it, or carries on with a conversion that was cut
               off
  Roll back    puts a conversion that was cut off back to FFS
  Stop         stops at the next safe point
  Save log...  the messages into a file

Convert first warns: back the partition up. Then it asks to go ahead, as
Roll back does. The run shows its step (n of 6), a progress bar with the
time left, and the messages. Keys: cursor up and down pick a partition,
A, C, R, S and L as underlined, Esc closes the window.

To convert the boot volume, copy ffs2pfs to RAM: and start it from
there; reboot when it is done. Disks with no partition in the DOS list
are not shown: use the Shell with DEVICE and UNIT for them.


In the Shell
------------

  ffs2pfs PARTITION [ANALYSE] [ROLLBACK] [PFS3AIO=file]
          [DEVICE=name UNIT=n] [NOREMOUNT] [LOG=file] [BOOT]

  ffs2pfs DH1: ANALYSE    checks the volume and the free space it needs,
                          writes nothing
  ffs2pfs DH1:            converts DH1:, or carries on with a conversion
                          that was cut off
  ffs2pfs DH1: ROLLBACK   puts a conversion that was cut off (power cut,
                          Ctrl-C) back to FFS
  DEVICE=scsi.device UNIT=0
                          finds the partition on the disk when it is not
                          mounted (a partition being converted has the
                          DosType F2P\0, which nothing mounts)
  PFS3AIO=file            puts another pfs3aio into the RDB instead of
                          the built-in 19.2
  NOREMOUNT               leaves the volume unmounted at the end
                          (otherwise it is mounted as PFS3,
                          write-protected until the next reboot)
  LOG=file                a copy of the messages
  BOOT                    converts the boot volume: copy ffs2pfs to
                          RAM:, run it from there, reboot when it is
                          done

Return codes: 0 done, 5 stopped by Ctrl-C (run it again to carry on, or
ROLLBACK), 10 failed but the volume is as it was, 20 error.

The conversion needs free space on the volume for the PFS3 reserved area
(FFS data blocks in it are moved out first), the plan and journal, and
the rollback copy. ANALYSE reports how much there is and how much it
needs.


If the power fails
------------------

Start ffs2pfs again. In the window the partition shows where it stopped:
Convert carries on from there, Roll back puts it back as it was. In the
Shell, run the same command again to carry on, or with ROLLBACK to undo
it.


Limits
------

  - The test suites use OFS and FFS (DOS\0, DOS\1). International and
    dircache volumes (DOS\2 to DOS\5) are accepted but not in the
    suites.
  - Blocks the FFS volume marked free may keep PFS3 data after a
    rollback. FFS never reads them.


